======================================================================
/etc/hosts FILE
----------------------------------------------------------------------
1. OVERVIEW
======================================================================
The /etc/hosts file is an operating system file that maps hostnames 
to IP addresses locally. It acts as a static lookup table that 
supplements or overrides external Name Service Switch (NSS) and Domain 
Name System (DNS) queries.

======================================================================
2. SYNTAX STRUCTURE
======================================================================
Entries are parsed sequentially using the following layout:

<IP_Address>    <Canonical_Hostname>    [Aliases...]

* <IP_Address>: IPv4 or IPv6 address.
* <Canonical_Hostname>: Primary Fully Qualified Domain Name (FQDN).
* [Aliases...]: Optional secondary short names separated by spaces or tabs.
* Comments: Any text following a '#' character is treated as a comment.

======================================================================
3. STANDARD TEMPLATES BY OPERATING SYSTEM
======================================================================

--- Linux / Unix / macOS (/etc/hosts) ---
# Localhost loopback addresses
127.0.0.1       localhost
::1             localhost ip6-localhost ip6-loopback

# Local machine hostname mapping
127.0.1.1       workstation.internal.local workstation

--- Windows (C:\Windows\System32\drivers\etc\hosts) ---
# Localhost resolution handled within DNS itself.
127.0.0.1       localhost
::1             localhost

======================================================================
4. PRACTICAL ADMINISTRATION SCENARIOS
======================================================================

A. Environment Overrides
------------------------
Direct traffic to a local development or staging web server without 
modifying public DNS records:

192.168.1.50    staging.example.com
127.0.0.1       dev.internal.local

B. Internal Infrastructure Mapping
----------------------------------
Assign readable aliases to internal cluster nodes, databases, or 
management interfaces:

10.0.0.10       db-primary.internal   db1
10.0.0.11       db-replica.internal   db2
10.0.0.254      router.internal       gateway

C. Null-Routing / Blocking Unwanted Hosts
-----------------------------------------
Block telemetry or malicious domains locally by routing them to a 
non-routable address (0.0.0.0 is preferred over 127.0.0.1 to avoid 
local port listener conflicts):

0.0.0.0         telemetry.vendor.com
0.0.0.0         adserver.malicious-domain.net

======================================================================
5. OPERATIONAL & MANAGEMENT COMMANDS
======================================================================

File Permissions & Ownership:
-----------------------------
Ensure the file is protected against unauthorized modifications.

Linux / Unix:
  sudo chown root:root /etc/hosts
  sudo chmod 644 /etc/hosts

Flushing DNS Cache:
-------------------
After making changes, flush the local DNS resolver cache to enforce 
an immediate update.

* Linux (systemd-resolved):
  sudo resolvectl flush-caches

* macOS:
  sudo dscacheutil -flushcache; sudo killall -HUP mDNSResponder

* Windows (PowerShell / CMD):
  ipconfig /flushdns

Order of Resolution (/etc/nsswitch.conf):
----------------------------------------
Verify local host precedence over external DNS in Linux:

hosts:          files dns

======================================================================
6. SECURITY BEST PRACTICES
======================================================================
1. Permissions: Restrict write privileges strictly to administrative 
   users (root / Administrator).
2. Monitoring: Monitor /etc/hosts for unauthorized alterations, as 
   malicious actors frequently hijack domain resolution to redirect 
   critical traffic.
3. Scope: Remember that modifications to /etc/hosts only affect the 
   host machine on which the file resides.
